1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,992
Two paints mixed into one new colour.

2
00:00:02,992 --> 00:00:05,575
Four scoops blended into one milkshake.

3
00:00:05,575 --> 00:00:08,107
Four perfectly equal bonds in methane.

4
00:00:08,507 --> 00:00:09,460
Three mixes.

5
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:13,244
In each one, different things
blend into equal new things.

6
00:00:13,694 --> 00:00:15,404
Diya sees it in the kitchen.

7
00:00:15,404 --> 00:00:18,857
She blends one scoop of chocolate
with three scoops of vanilla.

8
00:00:19,207 --> 00:00:21,195
Then she pours four glasses.

9
00:00:21,195 --> 00:00:23,652
Every glass tastes exactly the same.

10
00:00:24,152 --> 00:00:26,175
Atoms mix their orbitals the same way.

11
00:00:26,175 --> 00:00:29,591
By the end, you will know the
shape of a molecule from its mix.

12
00:00:30,241 --> 00:00:31,753
So what is the puzzle?

13
00:00:32,103 --> 00:00:35,032
Carbon's outer shell has one 2s
orbital and three 2p orbitals.

14
00:00:35,032 --> 00:00:36,822
They differ in energy and in shape.

15
00:00:37,172 --> 00:00:40,273
Yet methane has four bonds
that are exactly equal.

16
00:00:40,273 --> 00:00:42,875
Same length, same strength, same angle.

17
00:00:43,225 --> 00:00:44,565
Pauling gave the answer.

18
00:00:44,565 --> 00:00:46,879
While bonding, the atom
mixes its orbitals.

19
00:00:47,279 --> 00:00:48,830
This mixing is called hybridisation.

20
00:00:48,830 --> 00:00:50,724
The new orbitals are
called hybrid orbitals.

21
00:00:51,124 --> 00:00:55,082
Like the milkshake glasses, the hybrid
orbitals are all equal in energy

22
00:00:55,082 --> 00:00:56,049
and in shape.

23
00:00:56,699 --> 00:00:59,350
Let's watch carbon on our molecule bench.

24
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:03,162
At first, carbon has two
electrons in 2s, and two in 2p.

25
00:01:03,162 --> 00:01:04,549
Only two are unpaired.

26
00:01:04,949 --> 00:01:05,849
Step one.

27
00:01:05,692 --> 00:01:08,773
One 2s electron jumps up
into the empty 2p orbital.

28
00:01:08,773 --> 00:01:10,631
This jump is called promotion.

29
00:01:10,631 --> 00:01:12,579
Now four electrons are unpaired.

30
00:01:12,979 --> 00:01:13,879
Step two.

31
00:01:13,619 --> 00:01:16,027
The one s and three p
orbitals blend together.

32
00:01:16,427 --> 00:01:18,818
Out come four equal hybrid orbitals.

33
00:01:18,818 --> 00:01:22,160
One s plus three p, so
we call them s p three.

34
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:25,481
Each one is one part s and three parts p.

35
00:01:25,481 --> 00:01:28,331
So it has twenty five percent s character.

36
00:01:28,731 --> 00:01:33,310
They push as far apart as they can,
and point to the four corners of a

37
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:36,974
tetrahedron, at one hundred
and nine point five degrees.

38
00:01:36,974 --> 00:01:38,961
Each one overlaps a hydrogen.

39
00:01:38,961 --> 00:01:40,232
Methane is ready.

40
00:01:40,882 --> 00:01:42,386
Now mix fewer orbitals.

41
00:01:42,386 --> 00:01:43,908
Mix one s with two p.

42
00:01:44,308 --> 00:01:48,723
Three s p two orbitals, flat, at one
hundred and twenty degrees, like boron

43
00:01:48,723 --> 00:01:49,623
trichloride.

44
00:01:49,537 --> 00:01:51,789
One p orbital is left out, unmixed.

45
00:01:52,189 --> 00:01:53,656
Mix one s with one p.

46
00:01:53,656 --> 00:01:57,887
Two s p orbitals, in a straight line,
at one hundred and eighty degrees,

47
00:01:57,887 --> 00:01:59,327
like beryllium chloride.

48
00:01:59,327 --> 00:02:01,103
Two p orbitals are left out.

49
00:02:01,503 --> 00:02:02,819
Pause and think.

50
00:02:02,819 --> 00:02:08,462
Which hybrid orbital has the most s
character: s p, s p two, or s p three?

51
00:02:11,662 --> 00:02:12,562
S p!

52
00:02:12,248 --> 00:02:15,219
One part s out of two, so fifty percent.

53
00:02:15,219 --> 00:02:20,102
S p two has about thirty three
percent, and s p three has twenty five.

54
00:02:20,752 --> 00:02:24,059
Here are the rules of
mixing, one link at a time.

55
00:02:24,409 --> 00:02:25,309
Link one.

56
00:02:25,062 --> 00:02:28,412
The number of hybrid orbitals
equals the number of orbitals mixed.

57
00:02:28,412 --> 00:02:29,637
Mix four, get four.

58
00:02:30,387 --> 00:02:31,287
Link two.

59
00:02:31,114 --> 00:02:35,030
Only orbitals of almost equal energy
can mix, from the outer shell.

60
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:36,680
Link three.

61
00:02:36,607 --> 00:02:38,719
Hybrid orbitals make stronger bonds.

62
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:40,607
Their big lobe overlaps better.

63
00:02:41,357 --> 00:02:42,257
Link four.

64
00:02:42,057 --> 00:02:43,746
Promotion is not always needed.

65
00:02:43,746 --> 00:02:46,932
Full orbitals and half filled
orbitals can both join the mix.

66
00:02:47,682 --> 00:02:48,582
Link five.

67
00:02:48,350 --> 00:02:50,431
Hybrid orbitals point in fixed directions.

68
00:02:50,431 --> 00:02:52,365
That fixes the shape of the molecule.

69
00:02:53,115 --> 00:02:54,015
Remember this.

70
00:02:53,941 --> 00:02:56,644
Count the orbitals you mix,
and you know the shape.

71
00:02:57,344 --> 00:02:58,618
Now, one worked example.

72
00:02:58,618 --> 00:03:01,601
What is the hybridisation of
phosphorus in phosphorus pentachloride?

73
00:03:01,901 --> 00:03:02,801
Step one.

74
00:03:02,591 --> 00:03:05,316
Phosphorus makes five
bonds and has no lone pair.

75
00:03:05,316 --> 00:03:06,872
So it needs five orbitals.

76
00:03:07,622 --> 00:03:08,522
Step two.

77
00:03:08,235 --> 00:03:10,775
Its outer shell gives one
3s and three 3p orbitals.

78
00:03:10,775 --> 00:03:11,814
That is only four.

79
00:03:12,564 --> 00:03:13,464
Step three.

80
00:03:13,422 --> 00:03:15,249
So one 3d orbital joins in.

81
00:03:15,249 --> 00:03:17,030
One s, three p, one d.

82
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:18,680
Step four.

83
00:03:18,491 --> 00:03:20,426
That is s p three d hybridisation.

84
00:03:20,426 --> 00:03:23,529
Five orbitals point to the
corners of a trigonal bipyramid.

85
00:03:24,279 --> 00:03:28,231
Three bonds sit around the middle
at one hundred and twenty degrees.

86
00:03:28,231 --> 00:03:30,194
Two sit up and down at ninety.

87
00:03:30,194 --> 00:03:34,019
The up and down bonds are a
little longer, so they are weaker.

88
00:03:34,669 --> 00:03:37,597
Add one more d orbital, and we get six.

89
00:03:37,997 --> 00:03:40,922
One s, three p and two
d make s p three d two.

90
00:03:40,922 --> 00:03:45,160
Six orbitals point to the corners of an
octahedron, like sulphur hexafluoride.

91
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:46,908
Every angle is ninety degrees.

92
00:03:47,308 --> 00:03:49,116
Now count lone pairs too.

93
00:03:49,116 --> 00:03:53,143
In ammonia, nitrogen has three
bond pairs and one lone pair.

94
00:03:53,143 --> 00:03:55,533
Four pairs, so it is s p three.

95
00:03:55,933 --> 00:03:59,509
The lone pair squeezes the angle
to one hundred and seven degrees.

96
00:03:59,909 --> 00:04:02,919
In water, two bond pairs
and two lone pairs.

97
00:04:02,919 --> 00:04:06,436
Still s p three, at one
hundred and four point five.

98
00:04:07,086 --> 00:04:08,550
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:13,389
In ethyne, each carbon has four bonds,
a triple bond and a single bond.

100
00:04:13,389 --> 00:04:15,829
So students call the carbon s p three.

101
00:04:16,229 --> 00:04:17,129
Wrong.

102
00:04:16,836 --> 00:04:19,293
Pi bonds do not use hybrid orbitals.

103
00:04:19,293 --> 00:04:21,849
Count only sigma bonds and lone pairs.

104
00:04:21,849 --> 00:04:25,142
Each carbon has two sigma
bonds, so it is s p.

105
00:04:25,792 --> 00:04:27,078
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:27,078 --> 00:04:28,546
Both JEE Main and NEET.

107
00:04:28,546 --> 00:04:32,803
Finding the hybridisation of a central
atom, and matching it with the shape.

108
00:04:33,403 --> 00:04:34,409
Exam level now.

109
00:04:34,409 --> 00:04:37,910
In which pair do both central
atoms have the same hybridisation?

110
00:04:37,910 --> 00:04:38,958
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:44,358 --> 00:04:45,713
The answer is B.

112
00:04:45,713 --> 00:04:47,110
Water and methane.

113
00:04:47,610 --> 00:04:49,917
Methane's carbon has four bond pairs.

114
00:04:49,917 --> 00:04:51,793
Four pairs, so s p three.

115
00:04:52,293 --> 00:04:55,714
Water's oxygen has two bond
pairs and two lone pairs.

116
00:04:55,714 --> 00:04:58,272
Also four pairs, so also s p three.

117
00:04:58,772 --> 00:05:02,857
In A, ammonia is s p three, but
boron trifluoride is s p two.

118
00:05:02,857 --> 00:05:04,674
C is s p with s p three.

119
00:05:04,674 --> 00:05:07,376
D is s p three d two with s p three d.

120
00:05:07,376 --> 00:05:10,040
A traps students who forget the lone pair.

121
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,181
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:12,181 --> 00:05:13,765
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:16,965 --> 00:05:19,151
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:19,551 --> 00:05:20,451
One.

125
00:05:19,943 --> 00:05:23,272
How many hybrid orbitals form when
one s and three p orbitals mix?

126
00:05:24,072 --> 00:05:24,972
Two.

127
00:05:24,516 --> 00:05:27,535
Why does s p have more s
character than s p three?

128
00:05:28,335 --> 00:05:29,235
Three.

129
00:05:28,707 --> 00:05:30,556
What is the hybridisation
of carbon in ethene?

130
00:05:31,456 --> 00:05:34,910
Next time: why does liquid
oxygen stick to a magnet?

131
00:05:34,910 --> 00:05:37,509
We find out in molecular orbital theory.
